{
 "id": "2026-11-16-vulcan-amazon-leo-loss",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Forty internet satellites lost, ten minutes after liftoff. What does it mean for Amazon?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "On Sunday, a Vulcan rocket from United Launch Alliance lifted off from Cape Canaveral in Florida. About ten minutes in, ULA says, it suffered an anomaly and failed to reach orbit. All forty Amazon Leo satellites on board were lost."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Spaceflight Now reports the problem came during the burn of the Centaur upper stage. The cause is not yet known. It is ULA's first outright failure since the company was formed in two thousand six. The flight was meant to return Vulcan to service."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "It was the first of thirty-eight Vulcan launches Amazon bought. Reuters reports about four hundred Leo satellites were in orbit, of more than three thousand planned. The Pentagon was waiting on this flight before clearing Vulcan for military launches again."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Vulcan fly again before spring twenty twenty-seven? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
